# The shape the bug report actually hit: the module IS imported, but only for a
# value — the TYPE was left out of the import list. `stdlib.test` is imported for
# `check_true`; `TestState` is not, so the annotation below names a type that is
# declared in the bundle but not in scope here.
#
# This is the case that hides, because the file constructs no value of the type
# — nothing but the annotation mentions it. Constructing one locally gives a good
# diagnostic already (`Unknown record type or field`); annotating a value that
# arrives from another module gave none.
#
# Since the name IS declared somewhere, the error can say where it comes from.
import stdlib.test (check_true)

fn describe(s: TestState) -> String = "state"

fn main() -> Unit !{IO} = print("unreachable")
